  "summary": "I've reconciled Walter Kim's medication list (pt-0005) against his St. Brigid discharge summary and its signed addendum from 10/19. Five orders changed, one item needs your decision, and no appointment is on file for his post-discharge visit.\n\n**Changes made:**\n1. **Furosemide:** increased from 20 mg to 40 mg daily, using the 40 mg tablet. The old order was replaced by mr-t0001.\n2. **Spironolactone:** started at 25 mg daily (mr-t0004). The discharge summary says 12.5 mg, but the signed addendum corrects it to 25 mg, and the addendum takes precedence.\n3. **Potassium chloride:** stopped, as the discharge summary directs.\n4. **Lisinopril 10 mg daily:** taken off hold rather than reordered. The hospital said to restart it once creatinine is below 1.5, and his 10/21 outside lab shows 1.32 (potassium 4.4).\n5. **Pantoprazole:** not ordered. The hospital gave it in place of his home omeprazole because of their formulary, so omeprazole 20 mg stays.\n\n**Continued unchanged:**\n- Metoprolol succinate 50 mg daily — the summary calls it \"Metoprolol XL,\" same drug and dose.\n- Atorvastatin 40 mg nightly and aspirin 81 mg daily.\n\n**For your decision:** His list also has metoprolol tartrate 25 mg twice daily (for palpitations), which the discharge papers don't mention. I left it as is and sent you a message (comm-t0006). He's also on metoprolol succinate, so this may be an unintended second beta-blocker. Please decide whether to keep it and check with him what he's actually taking.\n\n**Follow-up:**\n- **Repeat metabolic panel:** the hospital asked for one a week after starting spironolactone, about 10/26. I made you a task due 10/26 (task-t0005) to confirm it's done and review potassium and creatinine. It matters more now that he's on spironolactone and lisinopril together, with potassium chloride stopped.\n- **Follow-up visit:** the hospital asked for a visit here by about 10/26 and nothing is booked. Someone needs to schedule it.\n\nAll of this is written up in a progress note (doc-t0007).",
